--- Overview
Reworks sync/syncsort.Sequencer so it supports bounded buffering and early termination, and so its internal goroutine can no longer block or leak when the consumer stops or the input stream ends with a gap.

--- Key Changes
sync/syncsort
  * Added WithMaxBuffered(n), an option that limits how many items can be issued by NextItem but not yet read via Scan. A token channel provides the backpressure, and n <= 0 keeps buffering unbounded.
  * Added Stop(), which can be called more than once and ends the ordering goroutine without cancelling the context.
  * Added Done(), a channel that closes when the ordering goroutine exits. Producers can select on it so their sends don't block.
  * Added the ErrStopped sentinel, which NextItem returns after Stop or after the goroutine has exited.
  * NextItem now takes a ctx and returns (Item[T], error). With a buffer limit set, it blocks until a slot frees up, either context is cancelled, or Stop is called.
  * Scan no longer returns a zero item on cancel, stop or end of stream. It returns false, and callers check Err() for the cause.
  * The ordering goroutine checks for cancel and stop before every send to scanCh, so it can't block forever on a consumer that has stopped reading. It closes scanCh and doneCh when it exits.
  * If the input channel closes while a sequence number is still missing, Err() now reports the expected sequence number and how many items were left, where before the goroutine hung.
  * With a buffer limit set, the initial heap capacity is min(maxBuffered, 100).
  * Fixed typos in doc comments and updated the README.

Tests
  * Updated the existing tests to the new NextItem signature and added goroutine-leak checks with synctestutil.AssertNoGoroutinesRacy.
  * Added tests for:
    * cancelling without draining
    * stopping early
    * bounded backpressure
    * worst-case out-of-order arrival, both bounded and unbounded
    * odd items arriving late and asynchronously
    * the input channel closing before all items arrive

--- Breaking Changes
  * NextItem(item T) Item[T] is now NextItem(ctx context.Context, item T) (Item[T], error), so every caller has to change.
  * When the context is cancelled, Scan now returns false without yielding an item. Code that relied on one last Scan() == true carrying the error needs to call Err() after the loop instead.
